From: SqueezeFS-Zig <sqz@localhost>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sqz: fuse: coalesce the contiguous pages of one folio into one descriptor in fuse_get_user_pages(), and release that many pins at once — a direct-I/O request over a large-folio buffer is pinned, dirtied, zero-copy registered and bio-mapped per folio, not per 4 KiB page

PROPOSED (scripts/kernel/proposed/, not in the series): compile-checked
(W=1, the whole fuse module: fuse.ko, cuse.ko, virtiofs.ko) as an
out-of-tree module against the 7.2.8 omarchy-sqz tree with 0001-0030
applied plus the proposed 0031 (it composes with 0031's per-entry
zero-copy scratch and stands without it: the hunks touch file.c alone);
NOT booted, NOT measured. The owner builds and boots; the probe plan is
below.

Why (docs/evidence/rung-10-seq-parity.md, wave 2). fuse_get_user_pages()
walks the pages iov_iter_extract_pages() returns and records one
(folio, offset, length <= PAGE_SIZE) descriptor PER PAGE — "Currently,
all folios in FUSE are one page" — even when 256 consecutive pages are
one 2 MiB THP folio (transparent_hugepage=always: fio's 1 MiB O_DIRECT
buffers here, any THP-backed application buffer). Every consumer then
works per descriptor, 256 times per 1 MiB request:

  - fuse_release_user_pages(): 256 unpin_folio() (and on a READ 256
    folio_mark_dirty_lock() + folio_unlock()) at fuse_request_end(),
    which under FUSE-over-io_uring runs on the daemon's ring thread at
    COMMIT — the thread that serializes a queue's whole depth;
  - the zero-copy install (fuse_uring_set_up_zero_copy): 256 folio_get(),
    a 256-bvec table copied into the imu (io_buffer_register_bvec: a
    kvmalloc + memset per request), and the release's 256 folio_put();
  - the daemon's READ_FIXED/WRITE_FIXED over that table: a 255-bvec bio
    whose bio_split_rw() segment scan, __blk_rq_map_sg() and
    sg_alloc_table_chained() are O(bvecs), then nvmet's 255 bio_add_page().

Measured on the workstation (RAM NVMe over nvme-loop, 1 MiB O_DIRECT
QD16 x 4 jobs, perf on the daemon's ring workers, 2026-10-20): on the
READ row these terms are 52 % of the worker's 46 us per request
(unpin_folio 15.2 %, folio_mark_dirty_lock + folio_unlock 7.4 %,
io_buffer_unregister 4.8 %, the imu memset 5.6 %, io_buffer_register_bvec
3.2 %, bio_split_io_at 6.1 %, sg_alloc_table_chained 4.6 %,
__blk_rq_map_sg 3.2 %, kmalloc 2.2 %) — about 24 us; on the WRITE row
25 % of 68 us — about 17 us (unpin_folio 5.9 %, io_buffer_unregister
3.8 %, memset 3.4 %, io_buffer_register_bvec 2.0 %, bio_split_io_at 4.8 %,
__blk_rq_map_sg 2.4 %, sg_alloc_table_chained 2.5 %). ext4 on the same
substrate pays none of it: iomap's bio carries the folio as one bvec.

What. In fuse_get_user_pages() a page that continues the previous
descriptor's folio contiguously extends that descriptor instead of
opening a new one, so num_folios counts folio runs (1-2 for a 1 MiB
request inside THP memory, 256 as today for 4 KiB pages); the page bound
(nr_pages against max_pages) is unchanged, so a request still carries at
most max_pages pages. fuse_release_user_pages() releases a descriptor's
pins with unpin_user_folio(folio, pages spanned) — GUP pins a large
folio once per page (gup_fast_pmd_leaf: refs = pages), so the release
gives back the same count; a one-page descriptor releases one pin, as
before. Every consumer already takes a (folio, offset, length) triple
whose length may exceed a page — fuse_copy_folio() (kmap_local_folio at
the offset; the page-cache descriptors are multi-page today), the uring
copy path through fuse_copy_args(), the zero-copy bvec (bv_offset past
the first page is the block layer's multi-page bvec form; io_import_kbuf
imports it with iov_iter_bvec + iov_iter_advance), virtiofs's
sg_set_folio(). Nothing changes for buffers of 4 KiB pages.

Expected saving (from the trace, THP-backed buffers): the daemon ring
worker's CPU per 1 MiB read -~24 us of 46 (-52 %), per 1 MiB write -~17 us
of 68 (-25 %); the imu allocation per request gone (1-2 bvecs fit 0031's
32-folio scratch); the DMA's bio one bvec, so the block layer's split
scan, sg mapping and nvmet's bio building are O(1) instead of O(255)
per request — a shorter device phase (the daemon's `store` phase, 485 us
at 52 GB/s against ext4's whole 300-460 us path). On the sequential rows
that CPU sits on the thread that serializes a queue's depth, so the
latency it costs is more than its CPU share.

Probe plan: the same rows (scripts/perf/matrix.sh sw1m-q16j4,
sr1m-q16j4, sw4m-q4j4) before and after the boot — the daemon's
`sqz-fuse-ur` cores and us/op from the row's .sched.txt, the
transport_held_write_phase_ns `store` and `to_commit` phases, perf's
unpin_folio share; then with transparent_hugepage=never (the 4 KiB
control: nothing may move); then the zc e2e rows, THE KILL MATRIX and
the fstests direct-I/O groups (generic/*dio*, a buffer straddling two
folios: the second descriptor's offset 0).
---
 fs/fuse/file.c | 40 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/fs/fuse/file.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/file.c
@@ -607,6 +607,17 @@
 	args->out_args[0].size = count;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The pages a user-page descriptor spans. fuse_get_user_pages() coalesces
+ * the contiguous pages of one folio into one descriptor, and GUP pinned the
+ * folio once per page, so the release gives back as many pins.
+ */
+static unsigned int fuse_user_desc_pages(const struct fuse_folio_desc *desc)
+{
+	return (desc->offset + desc->length - 1) / PAGE_SIZE -
+	       desc->offset / PAGE_SIZE + 1;
+}
+
 static void fuse_release_user_pages(struct fuse_args_pages *ap, ssize_t nres,
 				    bool should_dirty)
 {
@@ -616,7 +627,8 @@
 		if (should_dirty)
 			folio_mark_dirty_lock(ap->folios[i]);
 		if (ap->args.is_pinned)
-			unpin_folio(ap->folios[i]);
+			unpin_user_folio(ap->folios[i],
+					 fuse_user_desc_pages(&ap->descs[i]));
 	}
 
 	if (nres > 0 && ap->args.invalidate_vmap)
@@ -1629,13 +1641,29 @@
 			unsigned int offset = start +
 				(folio_page_idx(folio, pages[i]) << PAGE_SHIFT);
 			unsigned int len = umin(ret, PAGE_SIZE - start);
+			struct fuse_folio_desc *prev = ap->num_folios ?
+				&ap->descs[ap->num_folios - 1] : NULL;
 
-			ap->descs[ap->num_folios].offset = offset;
-			ap->descs[ap->num_folios].length = len;
-			ap->folios[ap->num_folios] = folio;
+			/*
+			 * A page that continues the previous descriptor's folio
+			 * contiguously extends that descriptor: one descriptor
+			 * per folio run, so a large-folio buffer costs one pin
+			 * release, one dirtying and one zero-copy bvec per folio
+			 * rather than per 4 KiB page. Every consumer takes a
+			 * (folio, offset, length) triple whose length may exceed
+			 * a page, as the page-cache descriptors already do.
+			 */
+			if (prev && ap->folios[ap->num_folios - 1] == folio &&
+			    prev->offset + prev->length == offset) {
+				prev->length += len;
+			} else {
+				ap->descs[ap->num_folios].offset = offset;
+				ap->descs[ap->num_folios].length = len;
+				ap->folios[ap->num_folios] = folio;
+				ap->num_folios++;
+			}
 			start = 0;
 			ret -= len;
-			ap->num_folios++;
 		}
 
 		nr_pages += nfolios;
